  hypothetical here — [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m already receives [38;5;153m{username, password}[39m as one object, and logging that object during a
  debugging session is the natural thing someone does at 2am. The allowlist makes that inert. The friction is real but
  it's a one-line change each time, and the console still shows everything locally.

  Separately, and regardless of which you pick: I will not log the password value under any option, and I'd drop the
  existing username from the [3mremote[0m payload in favor of a session id unless you want usernames queryable in your logs —
  tell me if you do, since that's a genuine debugging convenience with a genuine privacy cost.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What should the logger enforce about data reaching the remote sink? → Allowlist for remote (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246m· Should usernames be queryable in shipped logs? → No, session id only (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Two last things before I present the design.

  The repo has no linter, no formatter, and no test setup. A logger is exactly the kind of code that needs tests — the
  allowlist is a security control, and an untested security control is a hope. Tooling is also cheapest to add now,
  before there's code to retrofit.

  I also want to raise [1mglobal error capture[0m: hooking [38;5;153mwindow.onerror[39m / [38;5;153munhandledrejection[39m in the browser and
  [38;5;153muncaughtException[39m / [38;5;153munhandledRejection[39m in Node. This is where most of the "debug production issues" value actually
  comes from — errors nobody thought to log. It's the difference between logging what you predicted and logging what
  actually broke. I recommend it, but it changes the volume and shape of what reaches your collector, so it's your call.
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
← [38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ Error capture [39m[49m ☐ Tooling  ✔ Submit  →

[1m[38;5;231mShould the logger install global error handlers?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153mYes, both runtimes (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mHook window.onerror and unhandledrejection in the browser, uncaughtException and unhandledRejection in Node. [39m
     [38;5;246mCaptures failures nobody wrote a log call for, which is where most production-debugging value lives.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m Browser only
     [38;5;246mGlobal capture on the page where real users hit problems; the Node entry point logs only what it explicitly calls.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m No, explicit log calls only
     [38;5;246mThe logger records only what code deliberately logs. Predictable volume and no surprises in the collector, but [39m
     [38;5;246munanticipated crashes leave no trace.[39m
  [38;5;246m4. Type something.[39m
